        "problem": "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}=1.$",
        "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =-1,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,-1)$ and $(0,3).$",
